Correlate the rust target on the vendor, so rust 1.98 stops being ambiguous.

Mozilla's configure does not take the autoconf triple straight to rustc. It
runs "rustc --print target-list", groups the results by (cpu, endianness, os),
and narrows that group down to one rust target for the triple it was given.
For x86_64-redhat-linux-gnu -- which is what %configure passes as --host and
--target -- the narrowing that decided it was the raw OS, "linux-gnu", which
matched x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu and nothing else.

rust 1.98 added the OpenEmbedded targets, among them x86_64-oe-linux-gnu and
aarch64-oe-linux-gnu. Those share the raw OS *and* the raw CPU with the
generic target, so both narrowings now leave two candidates. The last step
tries the vendor, "redhat" names no rust target at all, and configure stops:

    ERROR: Don't know how to translate x86_64-redhat-linux-gnu for rustc

Nothing about the build root causes this: the same failure appears with the
Fedora rust and with an upstream-built one, and disappears on rust 1.94, which
has no *-oe-* targets.

Upstream has since taken the vendor correlation, in the same place and with
the same reasoning -- see the "*-oe-linux-gnu targets added in rust 1.98"
comment on mozilla-firefox main -- but only maps the "pc" vendor config.guess
emits onto rust's generic "unknown". A distribution triple carries a vendor
that names no rust target, so this adds the fallback that covers it: when the
vendor matches nothing, the generic target is the one meant. Every triple that
already resolved still resolves to the same target, on both rust versions.

diff --git a/build/moz.configure/rust.configure b/build/moz.configure/rust.configure
--- a/build/moz.configure/rust.configure
+++ b/build/moz.configure/rust.configure
@@ -302,6 +302,10 @@
         elif not candidates:
             return None
 
+        # config.guess uses the "pc" vendor for x86/x86_64 where rust uses its
+        # generic "unknown" vendor; normalize so we correlate on the right one.
+        vendor = "unknown" if host_or_target.vendor == "pc" else host_or_target.vendor
+
         # We have multiple candidates. There are two cases where we can try to
         # narrow further down using extra information from the build system.
         # - For windows targets, correlate with the C compiler type
@@ -402,6 +406,20 @@
             elif narrowed:
                 candidates = narrowed
 
+        # Correlate on the (normalized) vendor, so vendor-specific targets do not
+        # shadow the generic ones. rust 1.98 added x86_64-oe-linux-gnu, which
+        # shares its raw OS and raw CPU with x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, so the
+        # narrowing below stopped resolving. A vendor that names no rust target
+        # at all -- redhat, as %configure passes it -- wants the generic target
+        # rather than whichever vendor-specific one happens to come first.
+        narrowed = [c for c in candidates if c.target.vendor == vendor]
+        if not narrowed:
+            narrowed = [c for c in candidates if c.target.vendor == "unknown"]
+        if len(narrowed) == 1:
+            return narrowed[0].rust_target
+        elif narrowed:
+            candidates = narrowed
+
         # See if we can narrow down with the raw OS and raw CPU
         narrowed = [
             c
